  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Spotty showers possible this weekend, followed by \nrain Sunday night through Monday.\n\nA few passing shortwave troughs this weekend will bring the \npotential for some scattered showers both today and Sunday. The best \nchance is expected for this afternoon into the overnight tonight \nwith a slightly stronger trough crossing the region during this \ntime. For areas south of Lake Ontario, the combination of daytime \nheating and the passing shortwave trough will have the potential for \nproducing showers with a few embedded thunderstorms. Otherwise these \nareas will have plenty of dry time during the weekend. Areas east of \nLake Ontario will continue to have better chances for on and off \nshowers as remnants of the wind producing low from Thursday lingers \nover southeastern Canada today. The area is also closer to better \nforcing and closer proximity to the lingering trough associated with \nthe system. \n\nAn area of low pressure tracking across the Ohio Valley and toward \nthe Western NY area Sunday night will start to push showers into the \nfar southwestern portion of the area early Monday morning. Showers \nwill expand northeast across the area behind the systems passing \nwarm front. There is still uncertainty among the latest models with \nhow far north the shield of rain will make it. This will mainly \nbring into question if rain will get far enough north to reach the \nNorth Country, otherwise most of the guidance has rain pushing as \nfar north as at least I90. Current rainfall forecast is for half an \ninch to one inch of rain for areas south of I90, with the higher \nrain amounts toward the NY/PA border. Farther north, such as in the \nNorth Country, rainfall forecast amounts are closer to a quarter of \nan inch.",
